 For each site, domain coverage is estimated from the saved grid by
\begin{equation}
C=100\,\frac{\sum_{i,j} w_iw_j D_{ij}M_{ij}}{\sum_{i,j}w_iw_jD_{ij}},
\label{eq:angular_domain_coverage}
\end{equation}
where $D_{ij}$ indicates inclusion in the evaluated domain and $M_{ij}$ indicates that the human torque requirement is met. On each uniformly spaced axis, trapezoidal weights are one-half at the outer endpoints and one at interior samples; the common angular increments cancel in the ratio. Active-force coverage uses the positive-tension indicator in place of $M_{ij}$. For single-DoF routes, this becomes interval coverage; the repeated fixed coordinate cancels. These are grid-based quadrature estimates, rather than exact continuous-domain boundaries. Each site's fraction is normalized before the equal-site mean is calculated.

